\f1\fs22 Sarah knocked on the sliding glass door at the back of Chip Stockwell\'92s Sublime Point townhouse. When she was met with a lengthening silence, she put her hand against the glass to shield against glare and peered inside. The eating area and kitchen were a mirror-image of her townhouse, except for a wooden screen leant up against the stasis locker door that depicted a scene of skinny women and sumo wrestlers in acts of coitus in a panorama of snow-capped mountains.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Sarah wrinkled her nose and banged on the glass. \'93Mr Stockwell!\'94 More banging. \'93The barbecue has already started. I\'92m surprised you can\'92t smell it\'85 I mean there\'92s been a lot of smoke for a couple of hours now.\'94\
She considered calling security\'97Eloise would back her up if she had a door broken down and Chip dragged howling to the barbecue next door.\
The glass door slid open with fury. \'93What?\'94 its animator demanded.\
There stood Chip puffy from sleep, wearing a bad case of bed head and three-day growth, clad only in a dilapidated white terry towelling robe that almost but not quite failed to conceal a pinkish hairy chest and, a pair of baggy white underpants whose loose waistband elastic had retired from duty many years ago. Sarah stared at the sumo dudes on the screen and somehow found them less objectionable.\
\'93You\'92re late,\'94 Sarah stated, still looking anywhere but at Chip.\
\'93I\'92m not coming. Good morning.\'94\
\'93You already agreed. You told me you would come. Eloise told 
\f2\i you
\f1\i0  to tell 
\f2\i me
\f1\i0  you would come.\'94 Sarah lifted her eyebrows while they said, 
\f2\i And no one refuses Eloise Shakti.
\f1\i0 \
Chip pondered what to do, and chose aggression. \'93Don\'92t toy with me, girlie.\'94\
\'93Don\'92t toy with Eloise,\'94 Sarah answered, fearing his loose underwear were only one tired elastic snap from revealing the paucity of his toys. She gulped. \'93Besides, if those who have suffered greatly at your hand can offer you their peace and compassion, how can you obstinately remain the lesser man?\'94\
Chip sighed. He\'92d genuinely hoped the invitation was simply a social formality. How could the Chuglinghams want him in their home after all he\'92d perpetrated on them? How could they even stand to live next door to him? Although. the kids 
\f2\i did
\f1\i0  throw fireworks into his gutters at all hours of the night. More than once, they\'92d concussed possums whose subsequent deranged scratchings to avoid falling off the roof were more irritating than the bungers.\
\'93Fine,\'94 he conceded. \'93I\'92ll be there in ten minutes.\'94\
\'93I\'92ll wait for you.\'94\
Chip stared at Sarah until he was sure she wasn\'92t kidding, that she wasn\'92t going to let him off. \'93I\'92ll just put some pants on.\'94\
\'94That would be a great leap forward in neighbourhood diplomacy.\'94\
Chip vanished and returned seven or eight minutes later.\
\'93You could at least pretend to be looking forward to this,\'94 Sarah said as they walked to the back yard gate.\
\'94I am pretending,\'94 Chip replied. \'93Pretending I don\'92t know them, pretending this isn\'92t going to be the most excruciating social event since public executions went invitation-only.\'94\
\'93Well, the Chuglinghams have put all that behind them, so I\'92m glad that you can too.\'94\
Chip\'92s jaw went slack.\
Sarah smiled. \'93Good. Now be a gracious guest and eat whatever overpriced protein Mr Chuglingham inevitably cremates on his new ten-thousand-dollar barbecue.\'94\
\'93One hour,\'94 Chip said as they went through the gate. \'93Then I\'92m developing a sudden and severe migraine.\'94\
\'93Two hours,\'94 Sarah countered. \'93And you have to eat something.\'94\
They followed the circuitous path that arced away from the townhouse between wattle and under eucalypts. A short walk to their left, ochre cliffs and sharp blue sky met in a spectacular view of the kind that the Airborne paid obscene amounts to possess and Cloudborne only saw in advertisements for things they couldn\'92t afford. Chip gave no impression he appreciated any of it. Eventually, the path delivered them to the Chuglingham\'92s back gate, no more than twenty metres from where they\'92d left Chip\'92s yard.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Wearing an apron that showed fold lines from its original packaging, Balasubramaniam Chuglingham operated the control panel of a smoke-billowing apparatus that resembled a barbecue in exactly the same way the Hindenburg resembled a party balloon. Its stainless steel surface reflected the afternoon sun with almost vindictive brightness. He adjusted dials with all the acumen of a parrot at a submarine weapons console. He lifted the lid and peered. He moved steaks and sausages around the grill like they were pieces in a version of lightning chess that required extra speed because all the pieces were on fire. He put the lid down. He dialled and twiddled, and lifted the lid again. Clearly, he had cooked neither outdoors nor on a barbecue before; the Layer would do that to a Cloudborne lifestyle. The ever-increasing haze of smoke billowing around him carried the aroma of expensive meat suffering isolation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 At the sound of the back gate creaking open, he looked up from his tribulations.\
\'93Welcome, welcome!\'94 he called out, waving a pair of tongs like a conductor\'92s baton. \'93Sarah! And... Mr Stockwell, is it?\'94\
Chip gave a curt nod at Chuglingham\'92s pretence of meeting for the first time. Then their eyes met in a moment of mutual recognition that Sarah, mercifully, didn\'92t notice.\
\'93Ah, yes\'85 ah Bala, Super Cali\'85?\'94 Chip felt foolish.\
\'93Oh, I have an impossibly long name, is it? That\'92s ok, you can call me Balasubra.\'94\
Chip blinked and Mr Chuglingham laughed a genial laugh. \'93Bala is just fine, Mr Stockwell.\'94\
\'93Chip is just fine Mr Bala.\'94 Chip\'92s prodigious social skills always managed to desert him at times when social skills were not only required, but often life-preserving. Chip was still not sure which of those occasions this barbecue would be classified as.\
\'93Are you having a beer?\'94 Bala asked Chip, now they were on an abbreviated first name basis. He gestured at an esky in the shadows under the patio\'92s awning. It looked big enough to chill a whole deer, or possibly a moose.\
\'93Bala, careful with the temperature!\'94 Mrs Padmalakshmipriyamvada Chuglingham hurried across the terrace, a platter of raw meat in her hands. She was dressed in what Airborne catalogs would call \'93casual luxury\'94, clothes designed to look effortless while costing more than most Cloudborne crippling tax bills.\
\'93I have it under control,\'94 Bala assured her, pressing a button that caused flames to leap two feet high. \'93Just adding some\'85 dramatic flair. You see it is under control, Padmala.\'94\
\'93But it is not under control, Bala,\'94 Padmala complained, frankly out of sorts at being relieved of cooking duties.\
\'93Perhaps I could help, young Bala.\'94\
The three Sages had arrived silently and unnoticed, dressed in casual wizard hats, light open robes, board shorts and sandals. Each carried eskies.\
\'93Oh, Mr Kleo, I could not possibly\'85\'94\
Kleo let the half-uttered objection hang in the air a while then said, \'93It would be my honour. After all, one doesn\'92t simply become one of the Seven Sages without mastering the elements.\'94\
All but Solo and Chilo waited blankly for more explanation.\
\'93The four elements, one of which is fire, young Bala,\'94 Kleo explained.\
\'93It means, we can cook,\'94 Solo explained as Kleo relieved Bala of the barbecue tongs.\
\'93Oh, thank Murugan.\'94 Bala stepped back, removed his apron and offered it to Kleo.\
\'93Thank you, but I\'92ll manage in my own ceremonial barbecuing robes.\'94 Kleo set his esky down, opened it and fossicked about as he looked up at Bala and winked.\
A child\'92s wail pierced the afternoon calm. A boy of about seven had tripped on the garden path, his knee scraped and small amount of blood oozing.\
Padmala set down the platter and hurried to her son. \'93Tiki, what happened?\'94\
\'93I was looking up at the birds,\'94 the boy sniffled, pointing skyward. \'93Real ones.\'94\
\'93Fifth time today,\'94 Bala explained, abandoning his post at the grill. \'93They can\'92t stop looking up. Never saw an open sky before we moved here.\'94 Bala lifted his son to his feet while Padmala licked her fingers and dabbed at his wound.\
\'93Come meet our guests,\'94 Bala said and led his child for presentation.\
Alongside, Padmala said, \'93This is our son Tikirimanikandarajah. Say hello, Tiki.\'94\
\'93Hello.\'94\
Solo knelt before Tiki and examined his wound. \'93You know, Tikirimanikandarajah,\'94 he said playfully, while pronouncing the boy\'92s name perfectly. \'93I once scraped my knee during the capture of Salamis, and I\'92m still alive to tell the tale.\'94\
Tiki giggled.\
\'93And it\'92s worth it, to appreciate the beauty of the birds and sky, isn\'92t it?\'94 Solo asked.\
The boy nodded, a little embarrassed to be receiving such attention.\
\'93There\'92s a good lad.\'94\
While Solo amused Tiki, Chilo extracted a bottle of retsina from his esky and uncorked it, while Kleo made room on the wide stainless steel grill, shifting Bala\'92s tortured meats to cooler parts.\
\'93Don\'92t be alarmed,\'94 Kleo told Bala. \'93We can rescue those. They won\'92t have died for nothing.\'94 Bala didn\'92t notice Kleo\'92s boggling eyes communicating the exact opposite.\
Having made room, Kleo pulled a large octopus and a larger butchered haunch from his esky, which he threw on the grill with such vigour it shook.\
Bala regarded his steaks and sausages and sighed.\
Misconstruing Bala\'92s thoughts, Kleo said, \'93Well, I would have brought a live goat but Sarah assured me you don\'92t celebrate quite the same way around here.\'94\
As if to confirm Sarah\'92s counsel, Tiki ran to the barbecue with grief breaking out across his face. \'93Are they cooking a baby lamb, Amma?\'94\
Chilo regarded the boy with a condescending scowl. \'93It\'92s kid, not lamb,\'94 he corrected.\
Tiki wailed and ran for his life. \'93They are cooking kids, Amma!\'94\
\'93Oh, hush and don\'92t be such a child,\'94 Chilo called after Tiki. \'93In my day you\'92d get nothing but scraps after the soldiers had eaten, and that would be after a long day of collecting abandoned weapons and slaying wounded survivors on the battlefield.\'94\
Kleo took Chilo\'92s arm and spoke to his ear. \'93Keep your head down, Chilo. Sometimes I wonder if you forget what century we live in.\'94\
\'93I know exactly what century it is,\'94 Chilo asserted. \'93The last one.\'94\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The long patio table had transformed into a culinary United Nations. With the meats cooked and delivered to the table on the patio, Hayley and Padmala laid out plates of salads and jars of condiments. From their eskies, Chilo and Solo retrieved tied bundles of cloth that emitted aromas powerful enough to make the native wildlife reconsider their evolutionary dietary choices. Chilo untied his bundle of a selection of dried fish and olives and placed it out for all to share. Solo similarly unbundled barley bread, figs and feta, all divided into precise portions.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Kathy took a seat nearest the drinks table, under which she\'92d hidden her patches. Hayley would have sat beside her but recoiled from Chilo's anchovies long enough for Shyamala to claim it\'97the girl had been taking a curious shine to Kathy. Chip positioned himself at the intersection of multiple conversation zones, allowing him to appear engaged while participating in nothing. Sarah sat opposite, monitoring him.\
\'93So, Mr Stockwell,\'94 Padmala said, passing a bowl of Greek salad, \'93Sarah tells us you work with her now? Quite a change from your previous employment, I imagine.\'94\
Chip nodded tersely. \'93Better hours.\'94\
\'93I imagine so,\'94 Padmala said, spearing a sausage. \'93Those Fridge Force callouts could happen at any time, couldn\'92t they? Day or night. No warning for anyone involved.\'94\
\'93Padmala,\'94 Bala said quietly from farther down the table, \'93perhaps not while we\'92re eating.\'94\
\'93Oh, I\'92m just making conversation,\'94 Padmala smiled. \'93After all, we should know our neighbours, shouldn\'92t we?\'94\
\'93Padmala.\'94 Bala\'92s voice carried a warning.\
She set down her fork with deliberate care and raised her glass. \'93To new beginnings.\'94\
As glasses were raised around the table, the noisy back gate announced a new arrival.\
\'93Sorry I\'92m late,\'94 Eloise called as she advanced on the gathering, wearing hiking gear that appeared to have never encountered a trail more challenging than a department store escalator. Sarah stood to fetch Eloise a plate and a wine glass while she sat at the rather cramped end between Chip and Kleo.\
\'93Is everyone getting along?\'94 Eloise asked innocently as Solo poured wine for her. \'93I do hope everyone is being tactful about the past careers of others.\'94\
Padmala felt like everyone was staring at her.\
Chip never stopped eating.\
Bala could not make up his mind whether he should feel honoured or terrified.\
\'93For tax sake, Eloise,\'94 Kathy said from her end of the table, and raised a glass. \'93Take your finger off your button for once, will you?\'94\
The silence was only slightly quieter than a nuclear winter.\
\'93Is that your medical opinion, Kathy?\'94 Eloise asked icily, then laughed, reached across the table, grabbed Solo\'92s wine bottle and filled her glass to the brim. \'93I propose a toast,\'94 she said, \'93To people you can actually trust for a change.\'94 She raised her glass and tilted it towards Kathy.\
No one was sure whether to join the salute, and so the table went quiet.\
Until Tiki asked, \'93Is this the evil old warlord, Amma?\'94\
Padmala reached to enfold her son in protective arms while trying to stop herself from shaking.\
\'93Evil old arms dealer, darling,\'94 Eloise said to the boy. \'93Not a warlord. And if you play nice, I might buy you your first gun.\'94\
That only terrified Padmala more.\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Later, with lunch done  and the worst social disasters averted, Chip retreated to the very back of the yard. He found himself savouring the fresh Blue Mountains air. With his back to the party, he didn\'92t notice Bala Chuglingham approach. The snap of a twig spun Chip around and by then it was too late for Chip to slink away. There was probably a socially acceptable excuse he could make, but Chip lacked both the method and the skills.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 The silence between the two men stretched like overtaxed infrastructure. By the time Chip had figured this was just weird and perhaps he could walk away without any explanation, Sarah had pinned him there by a magisterial stare from the patio as she disengaged from whatever the inebriated Chilo was saying at her.\
\'93It\'92s so beautiful here,\'94 Bala finally said, gesturing at the trees with his beer bottle. \'93So\'85 fresh\'85 alive.\'94\
Chip studied his beer bottle.\
\'93You know, back in the city, under the Layer, we saved for years just to afford a screen that simulated this. A little window of fake sky that cost more than most Cloudborne make in a month. Now my kids see the real thing every day.\'94 He looked at Chip directly.\
Chip exhaled and said nothing.\
Bala took a long drink. \'93My daughter still wakes up screaming sometimes. From the detention centre.\'94\
Chip couldn\'92t look at him. \'93Look at where you are,\'94 he said. \'93Mountains. Real sky. I saved you.\'94\
Bala drained the last of his beer, turned the empty bottle over in his hands. Studied it. \'93Not by intent.\'94\
Bala\'92s daughter ran up, pointing excitedly at a grey bird perched in a scribbly gum standing just outside the yard.\
\'93Tatta! Tatta! Is that one real too?\'94\
Bala\'92s face transformed, the bitterness melting away. \'93Yes, Shya. That\'92s a real cockatoo. Not a projection. You see its red head and crest? It\'92s a gang-gang.\'94\
The little girl stared in wonder. \'93A gang-gang?\'94 She said, copying her father\'92s word for it. She giggled then dashed off to tell Tiki.\
\'93They sleep better here,\'94 Bala said to Chip. \'93Even with all we lost, they sleep through the night like nothing ever happened.\'94\
Chip wished he slept better.\
\'93Funny how things work out,\'94 Bala said. \'93Your lazy, authoritarian, punitive actions put in train a series of events that led directly to where I am today. Where my family is today. I\'92m in facilities management for this wondrous place. If you think these townhouses are grand, you should see Eloise\'92s mansion. And the research labs.\'94\
\'93I work in the research labs, Mr Chuglingham,\'94 Chip said. The fact still rattled him.\
\'93Of course, you do,\'94 Bala agreed.\
Chip felt a weight in his chest that beer couldn\'92t dissolve. Perhaps it was Kleo\'92s goat or too much honey and feta. \'93Mr Chuglingham, I\'97\'94\
Bala waved away whatever Chip was about to say. \'93I don\'92t need your excuses, or your rationalisations or your guilt. What would I do with it? Use it to pay for my children\'92s education?\'94\
From the patio, Sarah caught Chip\'92s eye and raised an eyebrow. He gave a small nod to indicate he was fine.\
\'93I can\'92t say I forgive you. But neither can I nurture any more animosity toward you, now we are in this place. Let\'92s just agree to be good neighbours. That is all I ask.\'94\
\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 As the evening progressed, the Chuglingham children convinced their father to light the fire pit at the edge of the garden. They gathered around it, faces illuminated by the dancing flames, while Bala told them stories about constellations they could now see without simulation.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 Chip stood apart, watching the family tableau with an unease he couldn\'92t quite name.\
\'93They seem happy,\'94 Sarah said, appearing at his side. \'93Despite everything.\'94\
Chip grunted.\
Padmala approached. \'93Mr Stockwell,\'94 she said, \'93it\'92s been enlightening. Do come again. We have so much more to discuss.\'94\
\'94Mmm,\'94 was all Chip could utter as he stared at Sarah, imposing on her the silent demand: 
\f2\i Never let this happen again.
\f1\i0 \
As he let the back gate slam behind him, laughter rose from around the fire pit. The Chuglingham children\'92s voices carried in the clear mountain air, delighted by something as simple as marshmallows toasting in flames.
